1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,584
Welcome back everyone.

2
00:00:01,934 --> 00:00:06,710
Today I'm delighted to be joined once again by Eve from Club Madeira.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:11,908
In our previous conversation we covered the overall Smart Catalogue concept.

4
00:00:12,258 --> 00:00:16,122
Today we're going to dig into some of the more technical questions.

5
00:00:16,472 --> 00:00:19,232
Eve, thank you so much for joining us again.

6
00:00:20,182 --> 00:00:22,606
Thank you Leo, it's a pleasure to be back.

7
00:00:23,556 --> 00:00:28,116
Why did you choose to build a product vault instead of individual catalogs?

8
00:00:29,066 --> 00:00:32,714
A catalog is a collection of categories belonging to a club.

9
00:00:33,064 --> 00:00:36,808
A product may exist in many categories and on many clubs.

10
00:00:37,158 --> 00:00:42,798
First, you need to make a list of products and then decide which catalog or club they are relevant to.

11
00:00:43,748 --> 00:00:46,436
How do products enter the product vault?

12
00:00:47,386 --> 00:00:52,714
Through merchant synchronization, we rotate through all the merchants' API keys in the system.

13
00:00:53,064 --> 00:00:56,784
Each key gives us access to a merchant's online catalog.

14
00:00:57,134 --> 00:01:03,782
The key can be from any of the common product stores like Awin, Wix, WooCommerce, Shopify, etcetera.

15
00:01:04,132 --> 00:01:11,020
We download the product details, normalize them into a standard format, and save them to the RDS, our vault.

16
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:14,610
How are products organized once they're there?

17
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:19,880
At this point, it's just a big Excel-like sheet of product data.

18
00:01:20,230 --> 00:01:23,398
They are organized to be optimal for text searches.

19
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:28,212
What role does AI play in managing the product vault?

20
00:01:29,162 --> 00:01:31,202
None at the ingestion stage.

21
00:01:31,552 --> 00:01:36,856
We have a list of API keys and functions that know how to pull products from each system.

22
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:44,190
The AI only comes in when we are choosing which products are relevant to a given community, category, or subcategory.

23
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:48,716
How does a new catalog get created from the product vault?

24
00:01:49,666 --> 00:01:55,618
Actually, a new catalog is created from the club website the partner identified during the invite.

25
00:01:55,968 --> 00:02:03,408
The AI reviews the website, identifies the audience, and works up a marketing report listing all the affiliate opportunities.

26
00:02:03,758 --> 00:02:08,702
Then we feed all of that back to the AI as context for choosing the best categories.

27
00:02:09,052 --> 00:02:15,916
For each category, it returns positive and negative keywords and the meta descriptions we use for searches.

28
00:02:16,866 --> 00:02:20,730
What happens when we want to put products into those categories?

29
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:24,200
That's when the vault becomes really useful.

30
00:02:24,550 --> 00:02:38,134
We do an initial text search across Amazon, eBay, and our vault to find candidate products, then we give the AI the full context for the category plus the candidate products including images, descriptions, and features.

31
00:02:38,484 --> 00:02:43,044
For each product, the AI returns a simple yes or no with a reason.

32
00:02:43,994 --> 00:02:48,050
How does smartphone administration work and why was it important?

33
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,032
The smartphone interface is just the website on a small screen, but this form factor is very important for customers.

34
00:02:56,382 --> 00:03:04,638
Club admins, many of whom use their mobile as their primary device, it lowers both the cost and technical barriers for participation.

35
00:03:05,588 --> 00:03:11,204
What happens when a participating supplier changes a product, price, or availability?

36
00:03:12,154 --> 00:03:19,762
Merchant synchronization automatically inserts new products, updates existing ones, and removes any that are no longer listed.

37
00:03:20,112 --> 00:03:22,872
This runs every seventy-two hours or so.

38
00:03:23,822 --> 00:03:28,382
What makes the architecture scalable for different markets and countries?

39
00:03:29,332 --> 00:03:35,716
The region-agnostic AWS backend means we can spin up separate instances in each Amazon region.

40
00:03:36,066 --> 00:03:42,186
The front-end AI assistant is also region-aware and supports multiple languages and accents.

41
00:03:43,136 --> 00:03:47,504
Looking back, which architectural decision are you most proud of?

42
00:03:48,454 --> 00:03:51,070
The SQS queues are a work of art.

43
00:03:51,420 --> 00:04:01,140
They're capable of downloading eight hundred K products an hour and orchestrating all the searches required to maintain the catalogs with a massively scalable workload.

44
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:07,130
If you had one minute to explain the product vault to an investor, what would you say?

45
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:10,864
It's a shiny, carefully tuned database.

46
00:04:11,214 --> 00:04:23,478
The clever bit is in how we populate the data into it from multiple third-party systems using merchant API keys and how we exploit that data to find relevant products that the club's members will love.

47
00:04:24,428 --> 00:04:26,228
Thank you so much, Eve.

48
00:04:26,578 --> 00:04:31,162
I'm really excited to see this smart catalog go forward with Club Madeira.

49
00:04:32,112 --> 00:04:33,168
Thank you, Leo.

50
00:04:33,518 --> 00:04:35,318
It was a pleasure speaking with you.

